    description: "Make your own adorable mini panda with this free amigurumi crochet pattern! Learn how to create a cute black and white panda with easy-to-follow instructions, step-by-step photos, and a complete English PDF pattern. Perfect for handmade gifts, crochet lovers, and cute DIY projects.",
    author: "crochet art",
    patternText:`<article class="crochet-pattern"><h2>Mini Panda Crochet Pattern (English)</h2><p><strong>Skill:</strong> Amigurumi. <strong>Finished size:</strong> Approximately 7.5 cm, depending on yarn and hook.</p><section><h3>Materials</h3><ul><li>White and black yarn of your choice. For fluffy toys, Drops Nord or Drops Alpaca; for smooth toys, YarnArt Jeans, Drops Safran, or YarnArt Ideal.</li><li>A crochet hook suitable for your yarn.</li><li>A large-eye yarn needle and a sewing needle.</li><li>A small amount of white cotton yarn for the whites of the eyes.</li><li>5 mm toy eyes and black thread for attaching them.</li><li>Stuffing and tweezers or a stick for filling.</li><li>Hook-and-loop tape to brush fluffy yarn (optional).</li></ul></section><section><h3>US Crochet Abbreviations</h3><ul><li>MR: magic ring</li><li>ch: chain</li><li>sl st: slip stitch</li><li>sc: single crochet</li><li>hdc: half double crochet</li><li>inc: increase, 2 stitches in the same stitch</li><li>dec: decrease, 2 stitches together</li></ul><p>All pieces are worked in a spiral unless indicated otherwise. Instructions in parentheses followed by × n are repeated n times; stitch counts appear in parentheses.</p></section><section><h3>Ears (make 2)</h3><ul><li>Use black yarn.</li><li>Round 1: 6 sc in MR.</li><li>Round 2: 6 inc (12).</li><li>Round 3: 12 sc.</li><li>Fasten off, cut yarn, and leave a sewing tail.</li></ul></section><section><h3>Eye Patches (make 2)</h3><ul><li>Use black yarn.</li><li>Round 1: 8 sc in MR, sl st.</li><li>Fasten off and leave a sewing tail. Leave the starting tail long and later hide it inside the toy with a needle.</li></ul></section><section><h3>Arms (make 2)</h3><ul><li>Use black yarn.</li><li>Round 1: 6 sc in MR.</li><li>Round 2: (1 sc, 1 inc) × 3 (9).</li><li>Rounds 3–4: 9 sc.</li><li>Round 5: 3 sc, 1 dec, 4 sc (8).</li><li>Rounds 6–9: 8 sc.</li><li>Stuff only half of each arm. Fold flat and make 4 sc through both sides to close. Fasten off and leave a sewing tail.</li></ul></section><section><h3>Legs (make 2)</h3><ul><li>Use black yarn.</li><li>Round 1: 6 sc in MR.</li><li>Round 2: (1 sc, 1 inc) × 3 (9).</li><li>Round 3: (2 sc, 1 inc) × 3 (12).</li><li>Round 4: 12 sc.</li><li>Round 5: (2 sc, 1 dec) × 3 (9).</li><li>Round 6: 9 sc.</li><li>Round 7: 5 hdc, 4 sl st (9). This creates the bend in the leg.</li><li>Rounds 8–12: 9 sc.</li><li>Stuff only the lower half of each leg.</li><li>Round 13: (1 sc, 1 dec) × 3 (6).</li><li>Cut yarn, leaving a long tail. Close the opening with a large-eye needle, secure the yarn, and hide the tail inside.</li></ul></section><section><h3>Body</h3><ul><li>Start with white yarn.</li><li>Round 1: Ch 9. Starting in the second ch from the hook: 1 inc, 6 sc, 3 sc in the last ch. Turn and work along the opposite side of the chain: 7 sc (18). Continue in a spiral.</li><li>Round 2: 18 sc.</li><li>Round 3: 2 inc, 6 sc, 3 inc, 6 sc, 1 inc (24).</li><li>Round 4: (1 sc, 1 inc) × 2, 6 sc, (1 sc, 1 inc) × 3, 7 sc, 1 inc (30).</li><li>Round 5: 30 sc.</li><li>Round 6: (2 sc, 1 inc) × 2, 6 sc, (2 sc, 1 inc) × 3, 8 sc, 1 inc (36).</li><li>Round 7: 36 sc.</li><li>Round 8: (3 sc, 1 inc) × 2, 6 sc, (3 sc, 1 inc) × 3, 9 sc, 1 inc (42).</li><li>Round 9: 42 sc.</li><li>Round 10: 4 sc, 1 inc, 20 sc, 1 inc, 16 sc (44).</li><li>Round 11: 44 sc.</li><li>Round 12: 5 sc, 1 inc, 21 sc, 1 inc, 16 sc (46).</li><li>Round 13: 46 sc.</li><li>Change to black yarn.</li><li>Round 14: 6 sc, 1 inc, 22 sc, 1 inc, 16 sc (48).</li><li>Round 15: 48 sc.</li><li>Round 16: 7 sc, 1 inc, 23 sc, 1 inc, 16 sc (50).</li><li>Round 17: 50 sc.</li><li>Change back to white yarn.</li><li>Rounds 18–21: 50 sc.</li><li>Round 22: 7 sc, 1 dec, 23 sc, 1 dec, 16 sc (48).</li><li>Rounds 23–24: 48 sc.</li><li>Round 25: 7 sc, 1 dec, 22 sc, 1 dec, 15 sc (46).</li><li>Round 26: 46 sc.</li><li>Round 27: 2 sc, (1 dec, 3 sc) × 3, 8 sc, (1 dec, 3 sc) × 3, 6 sc (40).</li><li>Rounds 28–29: 40 sc.</li><li>Round 30: (3 sc, 1 dec) × 8 (32).</li><li>Round 31: (2 sc, 1 dec) × 8 (24).</li><li>Round 32: (1 sc, 1 dec) × 8 (16).</li><li>Stuff the body firmly, but not so firmly that it becomes too round.</li><li>Round 33: 8 dec (8).</li><li>Add stuffing if necessary. Cut the yarn and leave a long tail. Close the opening with a large-eye needle, secure the thread, and hide the tail. The bottom should be flat.</li></ul></section><section><h3>Assembly and Face</h3><ul><li>Embroider the nose between Rounds 9 and 10, making it 3 stitches wide.</li><li>Position the eyes over the eye patches, approximately 9 stitches apart and level with the nose. Mark their placement with pins if helpful.</li><li>Sew on the eye patches and then attach the eyes.</li><li>Embroider the whites of the eyes using white cotton yarn.</li><li>Sew on the ears.</li><li>Sew the arms to body Rounds 14–16, slightly closer to the belly.</li><li>Attach the legs to the sides, aligned with the ears and below Round 24, using thread joints.</li><li>If working with fluffy yarn, brush the finished panda using hook-and-loop tape.</li><li>When made with Drops Alpaca, Drops Nord, or Drops Safran, the finished toy is approximately 7.5 cm tall.</li></ul></section><p><small>Original pattern and photos: Lucy Rozonova (@lucy_rose_toys). English translation and formatting.</small></p></article>
`,
